course: dictionary
lesson: 3782
title: Footage: seeing is not always believing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Footage means film or video recordings of an event. «CCTV footage» is video from security cameras, and «news footage» is video shown on the news. Footage shows real events, so it is a useful clue.
explain: But footage can be edited, cut, or shared out of context. «Old videos are often shared with new captions as if they were happening now; this is called recycled footage.» So a clip may be real but old. Always ask where it came from and when it was filmed.
explain: Footage can also help you test a claim. «Watching real footage, such as a nest camera, is a good way to test a claim about how birds behave.» If someone says birds do something, you can check the footage yourself.
fact: Footage means film or video recordings of an event.
fact: «CCTV footage» means video from security cameras.
fact: «Recycled footage» means an old video shared with a new caption as if it were happening now.
fact: Footage can be edited or even faked, so check where it came from.
fact: In old books, footage can also mean a length measured in feet.
exercise: «The camera caught the whole thing on footage.» What does footage mean here? | *Video recordings of the event | A pair of old boots | A length measured in feet | A photo of a crowd
exercise: Mia sees an old storm video with a new caption saying it is happening now. What is this called? | Behind-the-scenes footage | *Recycled footage | Nest footage | Measuring footage
exercise: What does «CCTV footage» mean? | A length measured in feet | *Video from security cameras | Photos of a crowd | Old captions
test: Why should you check where footage came from? | *Because footage can be edited, faked or old | Because footage only shows animals | Because footage is always a measuring tape | Because footage cannot be shared
test: What is a good way to test a claim about how birds behave? | Guess what you think is true | Read a poem about birds | *Watch real footage, such as a nest camera | Ask a friend who has never seen a bird
exam: True or false: footage is always true, so you never need to check it. | True: footage is always true | False, because footage only means a length measured in feet | *False: footage can be edited, faked or shared out of context | True, but only old footage
